{
  "dom_id": "step-q140415",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3x+12}{{x}^{3}+64}.$",
  "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3x+12}{{x}^{3}+64} & =\\frac{3(-4)+12}{(-4)^{3}+64}=\\frac{-12+12}{-64+64}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. \n\nNotice that the denominator is a sum of cubes. Therefore, factoring our expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x+12}{{x}^{3}+64} & =\\frac{3(x+4)}{{x}^{3}+{4}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(x+4)}{(x+4)({x}^{2}-4x+{4}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-4x+16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3x+12}{{x}^{3}+64} & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-4x+16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(-4)^{2}-4(-4)+16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16+16+16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
